.TH ONE-MORE-FFMPEG 1 "2026-11-13" "OneMoreFFmpeg" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
one-more-ffmpeg \- discover, download, install and inspect FFmpeg builds
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B one-more-ffmpeg
.RI [ --json ]
.RI COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B one-more-ffmpeg
.B --in-detail-usage
.SH DESCRIPTION
OneMoreFFmpeg is a CLI-first FFmpeg manager. stdout contains JSON when
.B --json
is used; diagnostics and first-use notes go to stderr. Write commands are
dry-run by default and require
.B --apply
to change files; download and capability cache writes are immediate.
.SH FIRST USE
No configuration file means built-in defaults. On first use a short note is
printed to stderr once and marked with <data-root>/.first-use. The data root
is controlled by
.B ONE_MORE_FFMPEG_HOME
and defaults to ~/.local/share/onemoreffmpeg on Linux. --in-detail-usage prints
the built-in full guide.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B version --json
Show build and runtime information.
.TP
.B detect --json
Discover FFmpeg installations.
.TP
.B validate --dir DIR --json
Validate one installation; always returns a report in data.status.
.TP
.B capabilities --dir DIR [--only KIND] --json
Run all capability probes, or only one list with
.BR --only .
KIND may be muxers, demuxers, devices, decoders, encoders, filters,
pixel_formats, channel_layouts or sample_formats. --cache-dir, --no-cache and
--refresh also apply to single-list probes.
.TP
.B release list --channel stable|daily [--days N] [--count N] --json
Stable versions are derived from https://ffmpeg.org/download.html#releases;
a 9.0 line is displayed as its latest patch (for example 9.0.2). Stable
always reports days=0; --count truncates the sorted versions. Daily
snapshots default to the last 7 days; --days and --count control the window.
When a snapshot asset name has no version, the snapshot tag is used.
.TP
.B release install [--version V|--snapshot TAG] [--allow-nonfree] --apply
Install the newest listed version or a selected version/snapshot.
--version and --snapshot are mutually exclusive; default is dry-run.
.TP
.B provider list|add ...
List or register providers. provider add is dry-run by default and needs
.BR --apply .
.TP
.B resolve --provider ID --channel stable|daily --json
Resolve a pinned release record.
.TP
.B download --release FILE --json
Download and verify the pinned asset. FILE may be UTF-8 or UTF-16 JSON.
.TP
.B install --release FILE [--apply] --json
Extract into a versioned directory, validate the trio and switch current.
.TP
.B uninstall --root DIR [--apply] --json
Move a managed installation into quarantine; reversible via journal rollback.
.TP
.B env status|set|unset|rollback
Inspect or update PATH/variables/shims. All modifying env subcommands are
dry-run by default and need
.BR --apply .
.TP
.B doctor --json
Check data root, current pointer, trio, PATH and pending journals. Always exits
0 with a report; scripts must read healthy.
.TP
.B journal list|rollback
List transactions or roll back install, uninstall and env transactions.
journal rollback is dry-run by default and needs
.BR --apply .
.SH SAFETY
nonfree builds require
.BR --allow-nonfree .
uninstall never removes unmanaged directories and quarantines managed ones.
Windows uses a directory junction for current; Linux uses a symlink; a
pointer file is the fallback. env writes are journaled with pending state and
can be rolled back. env set writes HKCU environment on Windows and shims plus
~/.profile on Linux; new terminals are required to see the new PATH.
provider add uses a cross-process lock around read-modify-write.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ONE_MORE_FFMPEG_HOME/config.toml
Optional configuration.
.TP
.I ONE_MORE_FFMPEG_HOME/providers.toml
Custom providers.
.TP
.I ONE_MORE_FFMPEG_HOME/apps
Versioned installations.
.TP
.I ONE_MORE_FFMPEG_HOME/journal
Transaction records.
.TP
.I ONE_MORE_FFMPEG_HOME/quarantine
Uninstalled installations awaiting rollback or cleanup.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success, 1 runtime error, 2 usage/config/contract error, 130 canceled.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR ffprobe (1),
docs/USAGE.md, docs/E2E_TESTING.md.
